{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And he said: Swear to me.</b> It was necessary for him to say to him a second time ״הִשָּׁבְעָה,״ and it was not sufficient with what he answered him to do according to his words, according to what we explained in the verse ״אָנֹכִי אֶעֱשֶׂה״ etc. It will be understood correctly that he is answering him regarding what he said — that he does not need an oath because he vindicates his words — rather, to do his will he asks him to swear, and this is his saying <b>swear to me</b> — meaning, to set my mind at ease, or for a reason hidden with me. And the intention is that he is concerned about others who might prevent him, as indeed was the case, for Pharaoh told him to release him from his oath, as they said of blessed memory (Sotah 34:), and therefore he immediately swore to him." "<b>Or</b> it may say <b>swear to me</b> — in my opinion, so that he not nullify the oath in his heart, for the essence of the oath is in the heart. And Jacob did so to secure against every side of doubt that might be in the matter."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:49:48.398901874Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}